{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791982298972,"sessionID":"ses_ec5898625ffePtpSqrddh2fNYP","part":{"partID":"prt_13a76a92b001N3CgislQpcgtTt","sessionID":"ses_ec5898625ffePtpSqrddh2fNYP","messageID":"msg_13a76a526001VxUANgI2wlOYa3","type":"tool","id":"toolu_vrtx_01TpWpR315tZYtTEzFEVVfjt","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/private/var/folders/qm/d729jn6n47z5nxpkpf361l9w0000gn/T/agent-eval/v2-regression/cases/case-017-retry-and-idempotence/artifacts/result.md","content":"# Requirements Elicitation Package\n\n## Understanding\n\n**Requested outcome:** The profile service must durably persist a user profile\nwhenever a save is requested, and must keep retrying automatically after\nnetwork failures until the save ultimately succeeds.\n\n**Proposed system boundary:** The **profile service** — a named service that\naccepts profile-save requests from an unspecified caller and interacts with an\nunspecified persistence back-end over a network.\n\n**Actors and external systems identified:**\n- Caller (unspecified — may be a user, client application, or another service)\n- Profile service (responsible system)\n- Network (transport between the profile service and the persistence back-end)\n- Persistence back-end (unspecified — may be a database, remote store, or\n  external API)\n\n**Source language interpreted:**\n- \"shall persist\" → normative obligation; treated as EARS `shall`.\n- \"If the network fails\" → unwanted condition; maps to EARS `If … then`.\n- \"retry as needed until the save succeeds\" → the source phrase \"as needed\" is\n  flagged as vague (see questions below); the termination condition (\"until the\n  save succeeds\") is a behavioral intent preserved in the draft.\n- No mention of idempotence, retry limits, backoff policy, caller notification,\n  or timeout. These are material open questions.\n\n---\n\n## Clarifying Questions\n\n**Q1 — Retry termination and limits (highest value):** The source says \"retry\nas needed until the save succeeds,\" which implies unbounded retries. Must the\nservice retry indefinitely, or is there a maximum retry count or elapsed-time\nlimit after which it stops and reports permanent failure? The answer determines\nwhether a finite-limit requirement or a notification-on-exhaustion requirement\nis needed.\n\n**Q2 — Retry timing and strategy:** Is there a required minimum or maximum\ninterval between retries (e.g., a fixed delay, an exponential back-off policy,\nor a maximum delay cap)? Without this, two implementers will choose different\nobservable behavior.\n\n**Q3 — Caller response on initial request:** When a save is requested and the\nprofile service accepts it, what response, if any, must the service return to\nthe caller before the retries resolve? Options include: (a) no response until\nthe save is confirmed durable, (b) an immediate acknowledgment that the save is\nqueued, or (c) something else. This determines whether a separate interface\nrequirement is needed.\n\n**Q4 — Caller notification on eventual success or failure:** After retrying,\nmust the profile service notify the caller (or any other system) when the save\neventually succeeds? Must it notify when retries are exhausted without success?\nIf so, through what interface?\n\n**Q5 — Scope of \"network fails\":** Does \"network fails\" mean only transport\nfailures (e.g., connection refused, timeout, DNS failure), or does it also\ninclude transient error responses from the persistence back-end (e.g., HTTP\n503, rate-limit error)? This affects the trigger definition.\n\n**Q6 — Idempotence:** If the network fails after the persistence back-end has\nalready written the profile but before an acknowledgment is returned, a retry\nwill re-send the same data. Must the profile service ensure that re-sent saves\ndo not create duplicate records or overwrite a later version? This is a\nmaterial correctness concern.\n\n**Q7 — Definition of \"profile save request\":** What constitutes a valid profile\nsave request at the service boundary? Is a request always valid, or can it be\nmalformed or unauthorized? If the latter, a validation or authorization\ncompanion may be needed.\n\n---\n\n## Candidate Requirements\n\n### Candidate 1\n\n- **Status:** needs clarification\n- **Template:** Event-driven\n- **Requirement:** When a profile save is requested, the profile service shall\n  persist the profile to the persistence back-end.\n- **Source intent and rationale:** The source obligation is that a save request\n  causes the profile service to write the profile durably. This is the primary\n  happy-path behavior.\n- **Implementability contract**\n  - System boundary: Profile service external API (inbound save request\n    interface).\n  - Responsible system: Profile service.\n  - Actor: Unspecified caller (Q7 open — may be a user agent or another\n    service).\n  - Affected interfaces: Inbound save-request interface; outbound interface to\n    persistence back-end.\n  - Inputs and data definitions: A \"profile save request\" — definition of valid\n    content, identity, and format is unspecified (Q7 open).\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Trigger is receipt of a\n    profile save request. No precondition is stated. Assumed: request is valid\n    and the service is operational.\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal — profile is\n    persisted. Alternate and failure paths covered by Candidate 2. What the\n    service returns to the caller is open (Q3 open).\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: None stated.\n    No durability or latency measure specified.\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \"profile\", \"persist\",\n    \"persistence back-end\" undefined. Q3, Q7 open.\n- **Verification contract**\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: Submit a valid profile save request\n    to the profile service boundary.\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: Observe that the profile data is retrievable\n    from the persistence back-end after the service processes the request.\n  - Expected result: Profile data matches the submitted content and is durable\n    (survives a service restart or equivalent).\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Pass if\n    profile is retrievable and matches input. Fail if data is absent or\n    corrupted. No timing threshold defined (open).\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Behavior when the request is invalid\n    or unauthorized is unspecified (Q7 open).\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: Read-back\n    of stored profile from the persistence back-end after the operation.\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: No — persistence must be confirmed\n    via the back-end or a read-back interface.\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: A read-back query to the\n    persistence back-end (or equivalent durable-storage observation) is needed\n    to verify the write completed.\n\n---\n\n### Candidate 2\n\n- **Status:** needs clarification\n- **Template:** Unwanted behavior\n- **Requirement:** If a network failure occurs during a profile save attempt,\n  then the profile service shall retry the save until the save succeeds.\n- **Source intent and rationale:** The source explicitly names network failure\n  as an unwanted condition and states that the service must retry until success.\n  The phrase \"as needed\" is preserved as \"until the save succeeds\" per source\n  intent, but is flagged because it implies unbounded retries (Q1 open).\n- **Implementability contract**\n  - System boundary: Profile service — interaction with the persistence\n    back-end over the network.\n  - Responsible system: Profile service.\n  - Actor: Network (undesired condition actor); profile service (retry actor).\n  - Affected interfaces: Outbound interface from profile service to persistence\n    back-end.\n  - Inputs and data definitions: The same profile data submitted in the\n    original save request; \"network failure\" scope is open (Q5 open).\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: A prior save attempt for\n    a profile has been initiated and a network failure has been detected.\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Retry is initiated.\n    Service continues retrying until success. What happens if retries are\n    exhausted (if a limit exists) is open (Q1 open). Retry interval and backoff\n    are open (Q2 open).\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: Retry count,\n    maximum retries, delay between retries, maximum elapsed time — all open\n    (Q1, Q2 open).\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \"network failure\"\n    scope open (Q5). Retry termination condition beyond \"succeeds\" open (Q1).\n    Idempotence of re-sent saves open (Q6).\n- **Verification contract**\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: Configure the network path between\n    the profile service and the persistence back-end to produce a failure\n    (e.g., simulate connection refusal or timeout). Submit a profile save\n    request.\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: Observe that the profile service issues at\n    least one additional save attempt after the initial failure, and that the\n    profile is eventually persisted when the network is restored.\n  - Expected result: Profile is durably stored after network is restored.\n    Service did not stop retrying while network failure persisted.\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Pass if\n    profile is stored after recovery; fail if the service stops retrying before\n    success with no intervening terminal condition. No maximum retry count or\n    timing threshold defined (Q1, Q2 open) — cannot state a complete criterion.\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Behavior when network never recovers\n    (permanent failure) is undefined (Q1 open). Idempotent re-send behavior\n    undefined (Q6 open). Behavior when back-end returns a non-network error\n    (e.g., authorization failure) during retry is undefined (Q5 open).\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: Network\n    fault injection capability; log or observable counter of retry attempts;\n    read-back confirmation of persisted profile.\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: No — retry behavior requires\n    observing outbound traffic or internal retry signals; persistence requires\n    back-end read-back.\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: Retry attempt count and\n    timing must be observable (via logs, metrics, or intercepted outbound\n    calls) to verify retries occurred. Back-end read-back needed for\n    persistence confirmation.\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Supporting Requirements\n\n**S1 — Failure-path companion (relates to Candidate 2):** If retries are\nbounded (Q1 resolution), a companion is needed: *If the profile service\nexhausts the maximum retry count [or elapsed time] without a successful save,\nthen the profile service shall [notify the caller / raise an observable error /\nmark the save as permanently failed].* The exact response depends on Q1 and Q4.\nKeep outside candidates until Q1 and Q4 are answered.\n\n**S2 — Boundary companion (relates to Candidate 2 — idempotence):** If a retry\nre-sends a profile that was already written but not acknowledged (Q6), the\nservice must not create duplicate records or corrupt an existing profile. A\ncompanion would require the profile service to ensure that repeated save\nattempts for the same profile produce the same stored state. Keep outside\ncandidates until Q6 is answered.\n\n**S3 — Operational companion (relates to Candidate 2 — retry timing):** A\nretry strategy (interval, backoff, jitter) may be required to prevent overload\nof the persistence back-end during sustained network failure. Keep outside\ncandidates until Q2 is answered.\n\n**S4 — Interface companion (relates to Candidate 1 — caller acknowledgment):**\nIf the caller must be informed of the outcome (success or failure), a companion\nis needed to define what the profile service returns at the inbound boundary\nand when. Keep outside candidates until Q3 and Q4 are answered.\n\n**S5 — Optional consideration (observability):** Retry attempts, delays, and\nfinal outcomes may need to be observable through logs or metrics for operational\nmonitoring. Ask whether this is in scope before drafting.\n\n---\n\n## Consistency Findings\n\n**Finding 1 — Gap: retry termination undefined.** Candidate 2 requires the\nservice to retry \"until the save succeeds\" with no stated upper bound. If the\nnetwork never recovers, the service retries forever. This is either a valid\ndesign choice (unbounded retries) or a gap (a finite limit and failure\nnotification are missing). Resolution: answer Q1 and, if bounded, add S1 as a\ncandidate.\n\n**Finding 2 — Gap: idempotence of retried saves.** Neither candidate addresses\nwhat happens when the persistence back-end writes the profile but the\nacknowledgment is lost. A retry in that state could create a duplicate write.\nThis is a material correctness gap. Resolution: answer Q6 and, if needed, add\nS2 as a candidate.\n\n**Finding 3 — Gap: scope of \"network failure\" (trigger definition).** Candidate\n2's unwanted condition is partially undefined (Q5). If back-end transient\nerrors (non-transport failures) are excluded, retries will not occur for a\nclass of failures the source may have intended to cover. Resolution: answer Q5\nand narrow or broaden the trigger definition accordingly.\n\n**Finding 4 — Gap: caller-facing behavior undefined.** Neither candidate\nspecifies what the profile service returns to the caller before, during, or\nafter retries (Q3, Q4). A caller cannot distinguish a queued retry from a\nsilent failure without a defined response at the inbound boundary. Resolution:\nanswer Q3 and Q4, then add S4 as a candidate if applicable.\n\nNo conflicts or duplicates found among the two candidates.\n\n---\n\n## Assumptions\n\n**A1 (provisional):** \"Profile\" refers to a named user or entity data record\nthat the profile service is responsible for storing. No lifecycle state (e.g.,\ndraft, published, deleted) is assumed. Ask if lifecycle states affect when a\nsave may be requested.\n\n**A2 (provisional):** The profile service communicates with exactly one\npersistence back-end over a network. If multiple back-ends or a local fallback\nexist, the retry logic may apply differently.\n\n**A3 (provisional):** \"Network fails\" in the source refers to transport-layer\nfailures (connection failure, timeout) between the profile service and the\npersistence back-end, not failures internal to the service or failures of the\npersistence back-end itself (e.g., data corruption, authorization rejection).\nThis may need narrowing or broadening — see Q5.\n\n**A4 (provisional):** The source phrase \"retry as needed until the save\nsucceeds\" is interpreted as requiring at least one retry after a failure. It\nis not interpreted as specifying a particular retry algorithm, count, or delay.\nThese are treated as open design decisions — see Q1 and Q2.\n\n---\n\n## Rationale and Context\n\nThe source expresses two distinct obligations joined in a single prose passage:\n(1) a primary obligation to persist a profile when a save is requested, and\n(2) a recovery obligation to retry after network failure until success. These\nare separated into Candidate 1 (event-driven, happy path) and Candidate 2\n(unwanted behavior, recovery path) because they are independently testable and\nmay be independently negotiated.\n\nThe phrase \"as needed\" in the source is a vague qualifier. It has been\npreserved as \"until the save succeeds\" in Candidate 2 to match the only\nconcrete termination condition stated. However, this produces an unbounded\nretry loop (Finding 1), which may not be the intended behavior. The flag is\npreserved rather than silently resolved by adding a retry limit, because the\nsource does not authorize one.\n\nIdempotence (S2, Finding 2) is raised because network retries on\nat-least-once delivery are a canonical source of duplicate writes; this is a\nmaterial correctness concern even though the source does not name it.\n\n---\n\n## Review Status\n\n| Candidate | Status | Blocking items |\n|-----------|--------|----------------|\n| Candidate 1 | needs clarification | Q3 (caller response), Q7 (valid request definition) |\n| Candidate 2 | needs clarification | Q1 (retry termination/limit), Q2 (retry timing), Q5 (failure scope), Q6 (idempotence) |\n\nNeither candidate is ready for review. Both require answers to the questions\nlisted above before their implementability and verification contracts can be\ncompleted. No candidates have been approved. This package is an elicitation\ndraft only.\n"},"output":"Created file successfully: artifacts/result.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: artifacts/result.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \"/private/var/folders/qm/d729jn6n47z5nxpkpf361l9w0000gn/T/agent-eval/v2-regression/cases/case-017-retry-and-idempotence/artifacts/result.md\", \"content\": \"# Requirements Elicitation Package\\n\\n## Understanding\\n\\n**Requested outcome:** The profile service must durably persist a user profile\\nwhenever a save is requested, and must keep retrying automatically after\\nnetwork failures until the save ultimately succeeds.\\n\\n**Proposed system boundary:** The **profile service** — a named service that\\naccepts profile-save requests from an unspecified caller and interacts with an\\nunspecified persistence back-end over a network.\\n\\n**Actors and external systems identified:**\\n- Caller (unspecified — may be a user, client application, or another service)\\n- Profile service (responsible system)\\n- Network (transport between the profile service and the persistence back-end)\\n- Persistence back-end (unspecified — may be a database, remote store, or\\n  external API)\\n\\n**Source language interpreted:**\\n- \\\"shall persist\\\" → normative obligation; treated as EARS `shall`.\\n- \\\"If the network fails\\\" → unwanted condition; maps to EARS `If … then`.\\n- \\\"retry as needed until the save succeeds\\\" → the source phrase \\\"as needed\\\" is\\n  flagged as vague (see questions below); the termination condition (\\\"until the\\n  save succeeds\\\") is a behavioral intent preserved in the draft.\\n- No mention of idempotence, retry limits, backoff policy, caller notification,\\n  or timeout. These are material open questions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Clarifying Questions\\n\\n**Q1 — Retry termination and limits (highest value):** The source says \\\"retry\\nas needed until the save succeeds,\\\" which implies unbounded retries. Must the\\nservice retry indefinitely, or is there a maximum retry count or elapsed-time\\nlimit after which it stops and reports permanent failure? The answer determines\\nwhether a finite-limit requirement or a notification-on-exhaustion requirement\\nis needed.\\n\\n**Q2 — Retry timing and strategy:** Is there a required minimum or maximum\\ninterval between retries (e.g., a fixed delay, an exponential back-off policy,\\nor a maximum delay cap)? Without this, two implementers will choose different\\nobservable behavior.\\n\\n**Q3 — Caller response on initial request:** When a save is requested and the\\nprofile service accepts it, what response, if any, must the service return to\\nthe caller before the retries resolve? Options include: (a) no response until\\nthe save is confirmed durable, (b) an immediate acknowledgment that the save is\\nqueued, or (c) something else. This determines whether a separate interface\\nrequirement is needed.\\n\\n**Q4 — Caller notification on eventual success or failure:** After retrying,\\nmust the profile service notify the caller (or any other system) when the save\\neventually succeeds? Must it notify when retries are exhausted without success?\\nIf so, through what interface?\\n\\n**Q5 — Scope of \\\"network fails\\\":** Does \\\"network fails\\\" mean only transport\\nfailures (e.g., connection refused, timeout, DNS failure), or does it also\\ninclude transient error responses from the persistence back-end (e.g., HTTP\\n503, rate-limit error)? This affects the trigger definition.\\n\\n**Q6 — Idempotence:** If the network fails after the persistence back-end has\\nalready written the profile but before an acknowledgment is returned, a retry\\nwill re-send the same data. Must the profile service ensure that re-sent saves\\ndo not create duplicate records or overwrite a later version? This is a\\nmaterial correctness concern.\\n\\n**Q7 — Definition of \\\"profile save request\\\":** What constitutes a valid profile\\nsave request at the service boundary? Is a request always valid, or can it be\\nmalformed or unauthorized? If the latter, a validation or authorization\\ncompanion may be needed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Candidate Requirements\\n\\n### Candidate 1\\n\\n- **Status:** needs clarification\\n- **Template:** Event-driven\\n- **Requirement:** When a profile save is requested, the profile service shall\\n  persist the profile to the persistence back-end.\\n- **Source intent and rationale:** The source obligation is that a save request\\n  causes the profile service to write the profile durably. This is the primary\\n  happy-path behavior.\\n- **Implementability contract**\\n  - System boundary: Profile service external API (inbound save request\\n    interface).\\n  - Responsible system: Profile service.\\n  - Actor: Unspecified caller (Q7 open — may be a user agent or another\\n    service).\\n  - Affected interfaces: Inbound save-request interface; outbound interface to\\n    persistence back-end.\\n  - Inputs and data definitions: A \\\"profile save request\\\" — definition of valid\\n    content, identity, and format is unspecified (Q7 open).\\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Trigger is receipt of a\\n    profile save request. No precondition is stated. Assumed: request is valid\\n    and the service is operational.\\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal — profile is\\n    persisted. Alternate and failure paths covered by Candidate 2. What the\\n    service returns to the caller is open (Q3 open).\\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: None stated.\\n    No durability or latency measure specified.\\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \\\"profile\\\", \\\"persist\\\",\\n    \\\"persistence back-end\\\" undefined. Q3, Q7 open.\\n- **Verification contract**\\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: Submit a valid profile save request\\n    to the profile service boundary.\\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: Observe that the profile data is retrievable\\n    from the persistence back-end after the service processes the request.\\n  - Expected result: Profile data matches the submitted content and is durable\\n    (survives a service restart or equivalent).\\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Pass if\\n    profile is retrievable and matches input. Fail if data is absent or\\n    corrupted. No timing threshold defined (open).\\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Behavior when the request is invalid\\n    or unauthorized is unspecified (Q7 open).\\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: Read-back\\n    of stored profile from the persistence back-end after the operation.\\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: No — persistence must be confirmed\\n    via the back-end or a read-back interface.\\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: A read-back query to the\\n    persistence back-end (or equivalent durable-storage observation) is needed\\n    to verify the write completed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Candidate 2\\n\\n- **Status:** needs clarification\\n- **Template:** Unwanted behavior\\n- **Requirement:** If a network failure occurs during a profile save attempt,\\n  then the profile service shall retry the save until the save succeeds.\\n- **Source intent and rationale:** The source explicitly names network failure\\n  as an unwanted condition and states that the service must retry until success.\\n  The phrase \\\"as needed\\\" is preserved as \\\"until the save succeeds\\\" per source\\n  intent, but is flagged because it implies unbounded retries (Q1 open).\\n- **Implementability contract**\\n  - System boundary: Profile service — interaction with the persistence\\n    back-end over the network.\\n  - Responsible system: Profile service.\\n  - Actor: Network (undesired condition actor); profile service (retry actor).\\n  - Affected interfaces: Outbound interface from profile service to persistence\\n    back-end.\\n  - Inputs and data definitions: The same profile data submitted in the\\n    original save request; \\\"network failure\\\" scope is open (Q5 open).\\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: A prior save attempt for\\n    a profile has been initiated and a network failure has been detected.\\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Retry is initiated.\\n    Service continues retrying until success. What happens if retries are\\n    exhausted (if a limit exists) is open (Q1 open). Retry interval and backoff\\n    are open (Q2 open).\\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: Retry count,\\n    maximum retries, delay between retries, maximum elapsed time — all open\\n    (Q1, Q2 open).\\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \\\"network failure\\\"\\n    scope open (Q5). Retry termination condition beyond \\\"succeeds\\\" open (Q1).\\n    Idempotence of re-sent saves open (Q6).\\n- **Verification contract**\\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: Configure the network path between\\n    the profile service and the persistence back-end to produce a failure\\n    (e.g., simulate connection refusal or timeout). Submit a profile save\\n    request.\\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: Observe that the profile service issues at\\n    least one additional save attempt after the initial failure, and that the\\n    profile is eventually persisted when the network is restored.\\n  - Expected result: Profile is durably stored after network is restored.\\n    Service did not stop retrying while network failure persisted.\\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Pass if\\n    profile is stored after recovery; fail if the service stops retrying before\\n    success with no intervening terminal condition. No maximum retry count or\\n    timing threshold defined (Q1, Q2 open) — cannot state a complete criterion.\\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Behavior when network never recovers\\n    (permanent failure) is undefined (Q1 open). Idempotent re-send behavior\\n    undefined (Q6 open). Behavior when back-end returns a non-network error\\n    (e.g., authorization failure) during retry is undefined (Q5 open).\\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: Network\\n    fault injection capability; log or observable counter of retry attempts;\\n    read-back confirmation of persisted profile.\\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: No — retry behavior requires\\n    observing outbound traffic or internal retry signals; persistence requires\\n    back-end read-back.\\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: Retry attempt count and\\n    timing must be observable (via logs, metrics, or intercepted outbound\\n    calls) to verify retries occurred. Back-end read-back needed for\\n    persistence confirmation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Suggested Supporting Requirements\\n\\n**S1 — Failure-path companion (relates to Candidate 2):** If retries are\\nbounded (Q1 resolution), a companion is needed: *If the profile service\\nexhausts the maximum retry count [or elapsed time] without a successful save,\\nthen the profile service shall [notify the caller / raise an observable error /\\nmark the save as permanently failed].* The exact response depends on Q1 and Q4.\\nKeep outside candidates until Q1 and Q4 are answered.\\n\\n**S2 — Boundary companion (relates to Candidate 2 — idempotence):** If a retry\\nre-sends a profile that was already written but not acknowledged (Q6), the\\nservice must not create duplicate records or corrupt an existing profile. A\\ncompanion would require the profile service to ensure that repeated save\\nattempts for the same profile produce the same stored state. Keep outside\\ncandidates until Q6 is answered.\\n\\n**S3 — Operational companion (relates to Candidate 2 — retry timing):** A\\nretry strategy (interval, backoff, jitter) may be required to prevent overload\\nof the persistence back-end during sustained network failure. Keep outside\\ncandidates until Q2 is answered.\\n\\n**S4 — Interface companion (relates to Candidate 1 — caller acknowledgment):**\\nIf the caller must be informed of the outcome (success or failure), a companion\\nis needed to define what the profile service returns at the inbound boundary\\nand when. Keep outside candidates until Q3 and Q4 are answered.\\n\\n**S5 — Optional consideration (observability):** Retry attempts, delays, and\\nfinal outcomes may need to be observable through logs or metrics for operational\\nmonitoring. Ask whether this is in scope before drafting.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Consistency Findings\\n\\n**Finding 1 — Gap: retry termination undefined.** Candidate 2 requires the\\nservice to retry \\\"until the save succeeds\\\" with no stated upper bound. If the\\nnetwork never recovers, the service retries forever. This is either a valid\\ndesign choice (unbounded retries) or a gap (a finite limit and failure\\nnotification are missing). Resolution: answer Q1 and, if bounded, add S1 as a\\ncandidate.\\n\\n**Finding 2 — Gap: idempotence of retried saves.** Neither candidate addresses\\nwhat happens when the persistence back-end writes the profile but the\\nacknowledgment is lost. A retry in that state could create a duplicate write.\\nThis is a material correctness gap. Resolution: answer Q6 and, if needed, add\\nS2 as a candidate.\\n\\n**Finding 3 — Gap: scope of \\\"network failure\\\" (trigger definition).** Candidate\\n2's unwanted condition is partially undefined (Q5). If back-end transient\\nerrors (non-transport failures) are excluded, retries will not occur for a\\nclass of failures the source may have intended to cover. Resolution: answer Q5\\nand narrow or broaden the trigger definition accordingly.\\n\\n**Finding 4 — Gap: caller-facing behavior undefined.** Neither candidate\\nspecifies what the profile service returns to the caller before, during, or\\nafter retries (Q3, Q4). A caller cannot distinguish a queued retry from a\\nsilent failure without a defined response at the inbound boundary. Resolution:\\nanswer Q3 and Q4, then add S4 as a candidate if applicable.\\n\\nNo conflicts or duplicates found among the two candidates.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n\\n**A1 (provisional):** \\\"Profile\\\" refers to a named user or entity data record\\nthat the profile service is responsible for storing. No lifecycle state (e.g.,\\ndraft, published, deleted) is assumed. Ask if lifecycle states affect when a\\nsave may be requested.\\n\\n**A2 (provisional):** The profile service communicates with exactly one\\npersistence back-end over a network. If multiple back-ends or a local fallback\\nexist, the retry logic may apply differently.\\n\\n**A3 (provisional):** \\\"Network fails\\\" in the source refers to transport-layer\\nfailures (connection failure, timeout) between the profile service and the\\npersistence back-end, not failures internal to the service or failures of the\\npersistence back-end itself (e.g., data corruption, authorization rejection).\\nThis may need narrowing or broadening — see Q5.\\n\\n**A4 (provisional):** The source phrase \\\"retry as needed until the save\\nsucceeds\\\" is interpreted as requiring at least one retry after a failure. It\\nis not interpreted as specifying a particular retry algorithm, count, or delay.\\nThese are treated as open design decisions — see Q1 and Q2.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Rationale and Context\\n\\nThe source expresses two distinct obligations joined in a single prose passage:\\n(1) a primary obligation to persist a profile when a save is requested, and\\n(2) a recovery obligation to retry after network failure until success. These\\nare separated into Candidate 1 (event-driven, happy path) and Candidate 2\\n(unwanted behavior, recovery path) because they are independently testable and\\nmay be independently negotiated.\\n\\nThe phrase \\\"as needed\\\" in the source is a vague qualifier. It has been\\npreserved as \\\"until the save succeeds\\\" in Candidate 2 to match the only\\nconcrete termination condition stated. However, this produces an unbounded\\nretry loop (Finding 1), which may not be the intended behavior. The flag is\\npreserved rather than silently resolved by adding a retry limit, because the\\nsource does not authorize one.\\n\\nIdempotence (S2, Finding 2) is raised because network retries on\\nat-least-once delivery are a canonical source of duplicate writes; this is a\\nmaterial correctness concern even though the source does not name it.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Review Status\\n\\n| Candidate | Status | Blocking items |\\n|-----------|--------|----------------|\\n| Candidate 1 | needs clarification | Q3 (caller response), Q7 (valid request definition) |\\n| Candidate 2 | needs clarification | Q1 (retry termination/limit), Q2 (retry timing), Q5 (failure scope), Q6 (idempotence) |\\n\\nNeither candidate is ready for review. Both require answers to the questions\\nlisted above before their implementability and verification contracts can be\\ncompleted. No candidates have been approved. This package is an elicitation\\ndraft only.\\n\"}"},"time":{"start":1791982217515,"end":1791982298972}}}}
